  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA cold front/occluded front is located from southwest MN to the\nborder area of northeast MN and northwest WI. Temperatures are\nheating up through the 80s on either side of the front, but dew\npoints are considerably higher in the warm sector where 70s and\neven some lower 80s are being measured. Heat indices are \napproaching or exceeding 100 throughout the Heat Advisory area.\nAs the front continues southeast and the base of the mid level\ntrough begins to swing through, thunderstorms are expected to \ndevelop and become severe. Various WoFS runs highlight north \ncentral and northeast WI and IA as the main target areas with\nminimum between these two areas likely due to the thunderstorm\ncomplex early this morning. In our area specifically, Rusk,\nChippewa, and Eau Claire counties stand the best chance for \nactivity going forward. However, this will depend how long it\ntakes for convective initiation to occur. If this takes another\nfew hours, it is likely to miss those counties as well. When\nstorms form they will be capable of all severe hazards.\n\nMuch drier air will continue working southeast into the evening.\nThe HRRR smoke model has hinted at some light smoke advecting in\non the northwest flow tonight, but upstream visibilities are\nnearly unrestricted. Some 8 and 9 mile reports, but nothing like\nthe past several days. High pressure will build overhead \nTuesday night. Highs in the upper 70s to low 80s and lows in \nthe 50s or 60s will bring much needed relief from the recent \nheat through Thursday. Ridging will return to the central U.S. \nnext weekend. The Upper Midwest will be on the northern \nperiphery this time instead of directly beneath it, so this may \nbegin a more active convective pattern heading into next week. \nThere will be some very hot air over the Plains that should \noccasionally move in. Highs by Sunday may be solidly in the 90s.",
